The forest projects, and a transform domain says so in drake

`ScenarioDrakeTest` established that the scenario can be SAID in today's drake. This is the other
half: does it MEAN anything? Hand each parsed `TypeDefinition` to `Generator.generate`, compile what
comes out, and then — because compiling is a weaker gate than it looks — ask whether what compiled
does the thing it claims.

    GATE 1  generate      22 of 22, nothing thrown
    GATE 2  compile       22 sources as ONE unit — cross-package references are the subject
    GATE 3  means it      an actor's REACHABLE rules, beside the ones its super-domain owns

Gate 1 passed on the first run. None of the four unprecedented constructs made the emitter throw:
`Ash_Birch` — a domain whose package tail deliberately disagrees with its name, carrying the
corpus's first underscore in a type name — emits valid Scala with no special handling at all.

Gate 2 opened at 12 errors and closed at 0, through two Generator fixes.

A BARE REFERENCE MUST REACH WHAT IT NAMES

    trait Potency extends Micromolar
    reference to Micromolar is ambiguous; it is imported twice in the same scope
    by import scenario.birch._ and import scenario.ash._

`derivationRef` qualified a parent only when it shared the DECLARING type's simple name. The
collision here is between two IMPORTED packages: the own-package chain and the derivation's. That is
not incidental to this corpus — it is what a transform domain IS, sitting under one species while
deriving from the other. `derivationRef` now asks whether the bare name would REACH the parent, and
"does package P hold a type named N" is a load probe, because a definition is a resource. The pinned
corpus does not move: 137/137 before and after. Two `type mismatch` errors went with it — they were
cascades, which the fix confirms rather than assumes.

A UNIT TYPE IS NOT A WRAPPER

Eight errors were one shape: `Encoder[scenario.ash.Micromolar]` not found. `Micromolar` derives
`Primal(Double)` and declares no elements of its own, so `codecDeclaration`'s `ownElementNames.nonEmpty`
guard withheld its codec — and `AshJasmonate`, which holds one, then had nothing to call.

The guard cannot be narrowed. `draco.base.Meters` is structurally identical to
`scenario.ash.Micromolar`; no predicate gives one a codec and withholds it from the other. Dev named
what the guard had wrong about them: **the purpose of a derived unit type is to identify a unit
conversion formula, not to structurally morph a type.** These are not wrappers that happen to sit on
a parent; the derivation exists so a conversion has two ends to name, and it adds no structure and
takes none away. So a unit type's projection is its parent's projection — which the corpus had
already settled, since every subtype of `TypeElement extends Primal[Json]` encodes `value` as a named
field. Guard dropped; `Type`, `Meters`, `Radians` and `format.json.Value` regenerated.

A DIRECTION IS AN ASPECT, NOT A DERIVATION

The scenario's transform domain had been saying transform-ness with a derivation — an experiment in
`from Transform(Ash, Birch)`. Three things argued against it. It is the move `05b14bd` had just
undone for actors, where a role's parameters were taken out of the data-inheritance tree. It asserts
something false: a transform domain holds no `Ash` value and no `Birch` value, only the conversions,
so the pair is metadata about the domain rather than its structure — which is why Scala cannot
project it (`Tuple2` is final) and why Haskell would have nothing to put in the fields. And drake had
no legal spelling for it anyway: `{K, V}` carries `Map` behind the operator, while `(S, T)` parsed to
a NAMELESS TypeName and emitted `extends [S, T]` — round-tripping by accident.

So `source` and `target` moved from `dracoAspect` to `domainAspect`, where a direction belongs, and
drake gained the two keywords:

    type Ash_Birch
    domain scenario ash birch Ash_Birch
      super scenario forest Forest
      source scenario ash Ash
      target scenario birch Birch
      types [ Potency Marker ]

Role is presence, applied to domains exactly as it is applied to rules and actors: a domain with a
direction IS a transform domain, and nothing appears in the extends clause, because nothing is
inherited. Both ends are required — one alone names no conversion. The slots had existed on
`DracoAspect` since before this session, populated by nothing and reachable from no surface;
`Generator.isDomain` already carried a `(source && target)` clause calling itself a transitional
fallback for transform-domain JSONs. It is not a fallback now.

The MEMBER conversions need no keyword and got none. `Potency from scenario birch Micromolar` with
`par potency scenario.ash.Micromolar` and the factor in the factory body already identifies its
conversion by its two ends — the same shape as `Meters from Distance(Double)`. That is the unit-type
formulation, and gate 2 now proves it projects.

WHAT GATE 3 IS FOR

Gate 2 closing at zero is exactly the condition under which the last finding would have disappeared.
`super` has NO projection: the only trace of it in `Ash.scala` is an unused import, and downstream
`RootInterface` builds a `Knowledge` that accepts nothing, because `actorKnowledge` reads the actor's
own domain and `Forest` is what owns the transform rules. It compiles, it runs, it matches nothing,
and no error anywhere reports it.

    ash/RootInterface.drake   own domain scenario.ash.Ash        reachable=0   []
                              super      scenario.forest.Forest  UNREACHABLE=1 [AshBirchAlarm]

That count is in the console headline, so it moves when the projection does. Left open deliberately:
whether a super-domain confers rule visibility is a decision about what `super` MEANS, not a defect
to be quietly patched.

A NOTE ON THE TEST

The Generator reads the definition GRAPH, not the definition — it follows the derivation chain to
decide whether a companion is a `DracoType`, reads a domain's member list to build an actor's
knowledge, and resolves inherited elements to emit a codec, all through `TypeLoader`. The scenario is
drake-only. So the test materializes the trio's normative member: each parsed `TypeDefinition` is
written as JSON to the classpath root it was loaded from, at the resource path its own `TypeName`
names, and removed again in a `finally`. Nothing under `src/` is touched. Without it every lookup
stubs out and the measurement is worthless.
